Kerio wrote:
> Then your problem is already solved - explore mode is NOT cheating.
> Cheating in NetHack is save scumming, wizbones-stuffing or rng
> exploitation, the explore mode was put in by the developers, to let
> people who are not familiar with the game progress anyway, to see what
> they have to do in a normal game - of course, don't try to pass your
> ascension as a legit one, because it's just not.

Indeed.  Don't think of explore mode as cheating.  It's there for you
to practice.  It'd only be cheating if it went on the high score table
(which it doesn't) or if you claimed it was a legitimate ascension.
(which you seem unlikely to do).  As long as you make it clear that
you were in explore mode, your conscience is clear.

I'd actually recommend that you take advantage of the "die?" prompt
here.  It's good to have some experience with the lower levels before
you get there for the first time in a real game.  Just make sure you
always play as though the prompt *isn't* there; if you work from the
assumption that death doesn't matter because of the prompt, the
practice will be worthless since you won't develop the right
strategies.  For the same reason, I'd recommend you not start explore
mode at the prompt; the wand of wishing changes the dynamic too much
for it to teach you anything about the early game, and without it,
you will know how long you would have made it in a real game, so it
gives you a good indication of your current skill level.

And now to contradict myself: it's also occasionally good to try out a
bunch of crazy strategies that will probably kill you.  Because you
can't risk that kind of experimentation in a real game, but in explore
mode, you might just make an interesting discovery.



